Apple is working on an activity tracker that doesn't have a screen, reports <em><a href="https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-09-22/apple-is-developing-new-fitness-tracker-aimed-at-rivaling-whoop">Bloomberg</a></em>. The screen-free health and fitness device is said to be similar <a href="https://www.whoop.com/us/en/">to Whoop's line of bands</a>. Whoop makes wrist-worn trackers with 24/7 monitoring, but without a display.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/09/whoop-band.jpg" alt="" width="1271" height="715" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1066769" /><br/>
Apple has apparently been "exploring" the category for months and has developed prototypes, but hasn't decided whether the project will move forward. Apple chair <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/tim-cook/">Tim Cook</a> and services chief <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/eddy-cue/">Eddy Cue</a> are interested in the product, notable because Cue now leads Apple's health teams. Cue is said to be pushing for wearables with simpler interfaces, like the screen-free Whoop and Oura Ring.<br/>
<br/>
Prototypes have a fabric band attached to a computing module that rests on the wrist. Sensors track heart rate and other metrics, similar to the Apple Watch.<br/>
<br/>
The latest Apple Watch models adopt features similar to those offered by Whoop and Oura products, including an overall readiness score and an estimate of biological age. A <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/09/16/ios-27-2-health-app-beta/">new version of the Health app</a> is being tested in iOS 27.2, and it has a more user-friendly interface with clearer guidance than before.<br/>
<br/>
